WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.890
Swirl a glass of wine, set it down on the table, and look at the inside of the glass.

2
00:00:05.890 --> 00:00:12.100
Untouched, the liquid climbs the wall and then slides back down in heavy drops.

3
00:00:12.100 --> 00:00:17.830
What drives this small display of defiance against gravity is not the quality of the drink.

4
00:00:17.830 --> 00:00:22.440
It is a quiet battle of evaporation going on at the rim of the glass.

5
00:00:30.640 --> 00:00:35.730
Set a glass of red wine on the table, swirl it a few times, and wait.

6
00:00:35.730 --> 00:00:43.700
Within a minute or two, a thin wet band appears on the inner surface of the glass, a few centimetres above the level of the liquid.

7
00:00:43.700 --> 00:00:48.790
The lower edge of this band thickens and ripples, and then drops break away from it.

8
00:00:48.790 --> 00:00:54.360
The drops slowly slide down to the bottom of the glass, leaving thin trails behind them.

9
00:00:54.360 --> 00:00:58.490
The band stays where it is, and new drops keep forming.

10
00:00:58.490 --> 00:01:01.340
This sight is called the tears of wine.

11
00:01:01.340 --> 00:01:10.750
In the English-speaking world they are called the legs of wine, and in Germany they are known as church windows, because the arches left between the drops look like Gothic stained glass.

12
00:01:10.750 --> 00:01:14.400
Something in this scene seems to go against gravity.

13
00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:19.970
The drops sliding down the glass are not surprising; gravity expects exactly that.

14
00:01:19.970 --> 00:01:25.220
What is surprising is how the liquid that feeds those drops got up there in the first place.

15
00:01:25.220 --> 00:01:31.430
The wine climbs the wall from the bottom of the glass, collects at a certain height, and only then falls.

16
00:01:31.430 --> 00:01:35.080
Why would a still liquid flow upward on its own?

17
00:01:35.080 --> 00:01:39.530
People must have been watching this sight for as long as they have been drinking wine.

18
00:01:39.530 --> 00:01:49.900
Some commentators believe the motion is mentioned in the Book of Proverbs, in the warning against wine that turns red in the cup and "goes down smoothly." That reading is disputed,

19
00:01:49.900 --> 00:01:54.030
but it shows how old the observation might be.

20
00:01:54.030 --> 00:01:59.760
The date of a scientific explanation, however, is certain: eighteen fifty-five.

21
00:01:59.760 --> 00:02:09.330
That year, the Scottish engineer and physicist James Thomson published a short paper on the "curious motions" seen on the surface of alcoholic drinks such as whisky and wine.

22
00:02:09.330 --> 00:02:15.380
Thomson was the elder brother of William Thomson, who would later become known by the title Lord Kelvin.

23
00:02:15.380 --> 00:02:20.950
He was a practical-minded engineer who worked on flowing water, turbines, and wheels.

24
00:02:20.950 --> 00:02:27.480
What caught Thomson's attention was that the motion on the surface had nothing to do with the liquid in the glass as a whole.

25
00:02:27.480 --> 00:02:33.850
It was happening along the line where glass and liquid meet, in a zone just a few millimetres wide.

26
00:02:33.850 --> 00:02:37.980
Thomson saw that the answer lay in surface tension.

27
00:02:37.980 --> 00:02:46.430
More importantly, he realised that surface tension alone did not explain it; the real driver was surface tension changing from one place to another.

28
00:02:46.430 --> 00:02:51.200
Wine is not a single liquid but largely a mixture of water and alcohol.

29
00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:54.850
These two components behave very differently from each other.

30
00:02:54.850 --> 00:02:58.660
And the secret of the tears lies hidden in that difference.

31
00:02:58.660 --> 00:03:07.750
To understand the idea Thomson put forward, we first need to ask a question: what exactly does the surface of a liquid pull on, and in which direction?

32
00:03:07.750 --> 00:03:14.280
Every molecule inside a liquid is pulled equally in all directions by its neighbours, so the forces balance out.

33
00:03:14.280 --> 00:03:20.650
Molecules at the surface, though, have no neighbours above them; they are pulled only from the sides and from below.

34
00:03:20.650 --> 00:03:25.260
This imbalance gives the surface a tendency to behave like a taut membrane.

35
00:03:25.260 --> 00:03:28.430
The surface wants to shrink as much as it can.

36
00:03:28.430 --> 00:03:33.200
That is why drops are round, and why some insects can walk on water.

37
00:03:33.200 --> 00:03:36.690
We call the measure of this tautness surface tension.

38
00:03:36.690 --> 00:03:40.820
Water has one of the highest surface tensions of any ordinary liquid.

39
00:03:40.820 --> 00:03:44.950
At room temperature it is about seventy-two millinewtons per metre.

40
00:03:44.950 --> 00:03:51.640
The surface tension of ethyl alcohol is a little less than a third of that, about twenty-two millinewtons per metre.

41
00:03:51.640 --> 00:04:02.650
When you mix the two, the resulting value depends on the proportion of alcohol in the mixture: the more alcohol, the lower the surface tension; the less alcohol, the closer it gets to that of water.

42
00:04:02.650 --> 00:04:11.420
A typical wine, containing between twelve and fourteen percent alcohol by volume, has a surface tension noticeably lower than that of pure water.

43
00:04:11.420 --> 00:04:16.510
Now suppose that the surface tension is not the same everywhere on a liquid's surface.

44
00:04:16.510 --> 00:04:20.640
Let one region be tauter and a neighbouring region slacker.

45
00:04:20.640 --> 00:04:23.650
You can think of it as a tug of war.

46
00:04:23.650 --> 00:04:28.580
The region with higher surface tension pulls the surface toward itself more strongly.

47
00:04:28.580 --> 00:04:32.710
The surface slides, dragging the liquid just beneath it along.

48
00:04:32.710 --> 00:04:39.720
So the liquid flows from the region of low surface tension toward the region of high surface tension.

49
00:04:39.720 --> 00:04:42.410
You can easily see this at home.

50
00:04:42.410 --> 00:04:48.140
Sprinkle a little black pepper on the water in a plate and touch the middle with a soapy finger.

51
00:04:48.140 --> 00:04:51.950
Where the soap touches, it lowers the surface tension.

52
00:04:51.950 --> 00:04:58.640
The tauter water surface around it pulls itself outward, and the pepper grains instantly rush to the edge of the plate.

53
00:04:58.640 --> 00:05:06.450
These flows, driven by differences in surface tension, take their name from the Italian physicist Carlo Marangoni.

54
00:05:06.450 --> 00:05:16.340
In eighteen sixty-five, in his doctoral thesis at the University of Pavia, Marangoni studied how a drop of one liquid spreads when placed on the surface of another.

55
00:05:16.340 --> 00:05:23.670
In the years that followed, he systematically investigated how differences in surface tension set liquid surfaces in motion.

56
00:05:23.670 --> 00:05:27.480
Thomson had laid out the basic idea ten years earlier.

57
00:05:27.480 --> 00:05:36.090
For this reason, some sources refer to the phenomenon as the "Thomson–Marangoni effect," but the name that stuck was the Marangoni effect.

58
00:05:36.090 --> 00:05:39.580
Let's apply this principle to the glass.

59
00:05:39.580 --> 00:05:44.670
When you swirl the glass, a thin layer of wine coats the inside surface.

60
00:05:44.670 --> 00:05:50.240
And where the liquid meets the glass, there is a meniscus that curves gently upward along the wall.

61
00:05:50.240 --> 00:05:55.010
This meniscus comes from the attraction between the glass and the liquid.

62
00:05:55.010 --> 00:06:02.500
In these thin regions the layer of liquid is very shallow, and the surface exposed to the air is very large compared to its volume.

63
00:06:02.500 --> 00:06:06.150
That is why evaporation happens so quickly there.

64
00:06:06.150 --> 00:06:12.680
Here is the crucial point: alcohol is more volatile than water, which means it evaporates more easily.

65
00:06:12.680 --> 00:06:17.770
In the thin layer, alcohol escapes into the air faster than water does.

66
00:06:17.770 --> 00:06:24.460
So the thin film of wine on the wall becomes slightly more "watery" than the wine in the middle of the glass.

67
00:06:24.460 --> 00:06:28.750
And because there is less alcohol, the surface tension rises.

68
00:06:28.750 --> 00:06:34.800
The result is a gradient on the glass, with higher surface tension above and lower surface tension below.

69
00:06:34.800 --> 00:06:37.330
Remember the tug-of-war.

70
00:06:37.330 --> 00:06:41.780
The tauter surface above pulls the slacker surface below toward itself.

71
00:06:41.780 --> 00:06:45.910
The surface slides upward and carries the liquid beneath it along.

72
00:06:45.910 --> 00:06:51.000
So the wine in the bowl of the glass climbs up the wall as a thin film.

73
00:06:51.000 --> 00:06:54.490
The energy for this climb comes from evaporation.

74
00:06:54.490 --> 00:07:01.180
Evaporation keeps drawing alcohol from the tip of the film, and so the difference in surface tension is maintained.

75
00:07:01.180 --> 00:07:04.670
The fresh wine arriving from below is rich in alcohol.

76
00:07:04.670 --> 00:07:09.600
As it rises, it too loses its alcohol and helps keep the difference going.

77
00:07:09.600 --> 00:07:12.290
But the liquid cannot climb forever.

78
00:07:12.290 --> 00:07:15.460
As it rises, gravity pulls it back.

79
00:07:15.460 --> 00:07:20.390
The liquid carried upward collects at the top edge of the film and forms a thick ridge.

80
00:07:20.390 --> 00:07:24.680
Once this ridge reaches a certain thickness, it becomes unstable.

81
00:07:24.680 --> 00:07:30.730
Instead of staying a straight line, it begins to ripple and breaks up into a series of separate bulges.

82
00:07:30.730 --> 00:07:37.260
When the bulges grow heavy enough, gravity overcomes the Marangoni pull, and droplets slide back down.

83
00:07:37.260 --> 00:07:39.950
These are the tears.

84
00:07:39.950 --> 00:07:46.800
The falling drop returns to the glass, but the ridge stays where it is, because the flow that feeds it carries on.

85
00:07:46.800 --> 00:07:50.130
There is a simple way to test this explanation.

86
00:07:50.130 --> 00:07:55.540
Cover the mouth of a glass with tears running down it, using a plate or a lid.

87
00:07:55.540 --> 00:07:59.670
Before long, you will see the tears slow down and then stop.

88
00:07:59.670 --> 00:08:10.520
The air inside the glass becomes saturated with alcohol vapor, evaporation almost stops, the difference in alcohol disappears, and the driving force behind the Marangoni flow is gone.

89
00:08:10.520 --> 00:08:15.290
When you lift the lid, evaporation starts again and the tears return.

90
00:08:15.290 --> 00:08:24.540
You can also compare it with a single non-alcoholic drink: grape juice swirled in the same glass simply runs down the wall and does not form a thick climbing band.

91
00:08:24.540 --> 00:08:27.710
Evaporation has a side effect as well.

92
00:08:27.710 --> 00:08:32.480
The evaporating liquid absorbs heat, so the thin film cools slightly.

93
00:08:32.480 --> 00:08:36.130
Cooler liquids generally have higher surface tension.

94
00:08:36.130 --> 00:08:40.260
This temperature difference adds a weak extra pull in the same direction.

95
00:08:40.260 --> 00:08:47.270
Even so, most researchers agree that the dominant factor in the tears of wine is the difference in alcohol content.

96
00:08:47.270 --> 00:08:51.080
For a long time, it seemed the matter was settled.

97
00:08:51.080 --> 00:08:58.570
Throughout the twentieth century, the idea of Thomson and Marangoni appeared in textbooks as an elegant example of surface forces.

98
00:08:58.570 --> 00:09:02.540
But it was also known that the explanation was not detailed enough.

99
00:09:02.540 --> 00:09:05.710
Why does the film stop at a certain height?

100
00:09:05.710 --> 00:09:09.840
Why does the ridge break into droplets at regular intervals?

101
00:09:09.840 --> 00:09:12.850
What role does swirling the glass play?

102
00:09:12.850 --> 00:09:18.260
The answers to these questions came from a rather exotic corner of fluid mathematics.

103
00:09:18.260 --> 00:09:26.870
In two thousand one, Anette Hosoi and John Bush of the Massachusetts Institute of Technology published a study on climbing films driven by evaporation.

104
00:09:26.870 --> 00:09:33.080
They showed in detail that the droplets arise from an instability that develops in the ridge at the front of the film.

105
00:09:33.080 --> 00:09:43.130
But the most striking part of the story came about twenty years later, from the applied mathematician Andrea Bertozzi and her team at the University of California, Los Angeles.

106
00:09:43.130 --> 00:09:50.620
Together with Yonatan Dukler, Hangjie Ji and Claudia Falcon, Bertozzi built a mathematical model of the climbing film.

107
00:09:50.620 --> 00:10:00.350
They presented their results at the end of twenty nineteen at the American Physical Society's meeting on fluid dynamics, and published them in twenty twenty in the society's fluids journal.

108
00:10:00.350 --> 00:10:06.880
Their conclusion was this: the leading edge of the wine climbing the wall of the glass is actually a shock wave.

109
00:10:06.880 --> 00:10:13.250
The phrase "shock wave" brings to mind the boom of a plane breaking the speed of sound, or a supernova.

110
00:10:13.250 --> 00:10:16.420
In mathematics, its meaning is broader.

111
00:10:16.420 --> 00:10:22.470
A shock is a front where the properties of a medium change almost in a leap, within a very narrow region.

112
00:10:22.470 --> 00:10:29.640
In the glass, this is the sharp transition where the thin film is very thin on one side and much thicker on the other.

113
00:10:29.640 --> 00:10:36.010
In equations of this kind, shocks also move with a speed and a direction, and there are rules that govern them.

114
00:10:36.010 --> 00:10:44.460
In ordinary shocks, information flows into the front from both sides: the regions ahead of and behind the wave "collide" with the front and compress it.

115
00:10:44.460 --> 00:10:49.550
The shock that Bertozzi's team found in the wine film was of a different kind.

116
00:10:49.550 --> 00:10:53.680
It was what mathematicians call an "undercompressive" shock.

117
00:10:53.680 --> 00:10:58.290
In these shocks, information does not arrive at the front from both sides.

118
00:10:58.290 --> 00:11:08.020
The front advances behind a ridge of its own making, and its structure depends not only on the conditions on either side, but also on the delicate physics within the front itself,

119
00:11:08.020 --> 00:11:11.990
namely surface tension and the curvature of the film.

120
00:11:11.990 --> 00:11:18.360
Shocks of this kind are rare in nature and are especially intriguing structures from a mathematical point of view.

121
00:11:18.360 --> 00:11:25.690
Bertozzi's group had studied them before, in earlier work on thin films driven up an inclined surface by Marangoni stress.

122
00:11:25.690 --> 00:11:31.100
This time, they showed that the same kind of structure forms every evening at dinner tables.

123
00:11:31.100 --> 00:11:37.950
According to the team's model, the shock in the wine's climb is a front that advances upward, against gravity.

124
00:11:37.950 --> 00:11:42.080
The droplets form when the ridge ahead of this front becomes unstable.

125
00:11:42.080 --> 00:11:46.210
The model also explained another everyday detail.

126
00:11:46.210 --> 00:11:51.300
Swirling the glass is not just a tasting ritual; it is part of the physics, too.

127
00:11:51.300 --> 00:11:56.070
Swirling pre-wets the inside of the glass with a thin film of wine.

128
00:11:56.070 --> 00:12:04.360
Bertozzi's team showed that the behavior of the climbing front depends on the thickness of this pre-coated film and on how high the liquid rises.

129
00:12:04.360 --> 00:12:15.050
The team also ran experiments: they swirled mixtures with different alcohol contents in glasses, watched the climbing front, and found that their observations matched the model's predictions.

130
00:12:15.050 --> 00:12:22.060
The sight in an ordinary wine glass was a living demonstration of one of the subtlest consequences of thin-film equations.

131
00:12:22.060 --> 00:12:29.230
This scientific picture also has an everyday consequence, and it contradicts a belief that has long lived on in wine culture.

132
00:12:29.230 --> 00:12:36.240
Many wine lovers believe that a wine leaving pronounced, slow-moving tears in the glass is "fuller," or of higher quality.

133
00:12:36.240 --> 00:12:41.970
In some tasting settings, the tears are even read as a clue to a wine's body and age.

134
00:12:41.970 --> 00:12:46.900
Another common explanation is that the tears come from the glycerol or sugar in the wine.

135
00:12:46.900 --> 00:12:52.470
Glycerol is a syrupy, viscous compound produced as a by-product of fermentation.

136
00:12:52.470 --> 00:12:55.320
Physics does not support these beliefs.

137
00:12:55.320 --> 00:13:01.850
The engine that creates the tears is the difference in evaporation and surface tension between alcohol and water.

138
00:13:01.850 --> 00:13:06.940
So what most determines how strong the tears are is the wine's alcohol content.

139
00:13:06.940 --> 00:13:15.550
As the alcohol rises, the surface tension difference driven by evaporation generally becomes more pronounced, and the tears become more visible.

140
00:13:15.550 --> 00:13:24.160
The surroundings matter as well: in a dry, warm room, evaporation is fast; in a damp, cool cellar, it is slow.

141
00:13:24.160 --> 00:13:30.530
How clean the glass is, and whether it carries traces of dish detergent, also changes the outcome.

142
00:13:30.530 --> 00:13:38.340
Glycerol and sugar can alter the liquid's viscosity slightly and slow the flow of the droplets, but they are not the source of the tears.

143
00:13:38.340 --> 00:13:45.030
A mixture of water and alcohol, entirely free of glycerol, also produces flawless tears.

144
00:13:45.030 --> 00:13:52.680
Bertozzi underlined this point when explaining the results: the tears tell you about the alcohol, not about the quality of the wine.

145
00:13:52.680 --> 00:13:56.170
This does not mean that tasting is meaningless.

146
00:13:56.170 --> 00:14:01.580
A wine's color, aroma, and structure on the palate say a great deal about it.

147
00:14:01.580 --> 00:14:07.950
The tears tell you something too, but that something is not quality; it is alcohol content.

148
00:14:07.950 --> 00:14:14.320
A cheap wine with high alcohol can leave more impressive tears than an expensive but light one.

149
00:14:14.320 --> 00:14:19.090
The sight in the glass is beautiful, but it is no certificate of quality.

150
00:14:19.090 --> 00:14:25.620
Perhaps the most striking part of this story is how widely the principle behind this little show in the glass is put to work.

151
00:14:25.620 --> 00:14:32.150
Today the Marangoni effect is not merely a curiosity, but a tool that engineers use deliberately.

152
00:14:32.150 --> 00:14:42.680
In microchip manufacturing, silicon wafers must be dried perfectly after every rinsing step, because even a single droplet drying on the surface can leave marks and contamination.

153
00:14:42.680 --> 00:14:53.690
In a method that became widespread in the nineteen-nineties and takes its name directly from Marangoni, isopropyl alcohol vapor is directed onto the wafer's surface as it is drawn out of the water.

154
00:14:53.690 --> 00:14:58.460
This vapor lowers the surface tension along the line where the wafer meets the water.

155
00:14:58.460 --> 00:15:06.750
The resulting Marangoni flow pulls the water back off the wafer's surface, toward the bulk of the liquid, and the wafer emerges almost bone dry.

156
00:15:06.750 --> 00:15:17.760
This is the phenomenon in the wine glass applied in reverse: in wine, the surface tension difference pulls the liquid up along the glass, while in wafer drying, it draws the water away from the surface.

157
00:15:17.760 --> 00:15:24.130
The same principle is also being studied to understand the stains that drying droplets leave behind.

158
00:15:24.130 --> 00:15:33.060
In inkjet printing, coating technologies, and medical diagnostic tests, the way a droplet dries determines how uniform the final product will be.

159
00:15:33.060 --> 00:15:39.590
Steering these flows by manipulating surface tension differences is an active area of engineering research.

160
00:15:39.590 --> 00:15:51.240
Marangoni flows driven by temperature differences, meanwhile, are being studied as an important factor shaping the pool of molten metal during welding, and the behavior of liquids aboard space stations,

161
00:15:51.240 --> 00:15:53.770
where gravity is barely felt.

162
00:15:53.770 --> 00:16:00.460
There, because gravity-driven flows all but vanish, surface tension differences run the show on their own.

163
00:16:00.460 --> 00:16:06.830
When James Thomson gazed into his glass in eighteen fifty-five, he could not have foreseen any of this.

164
00:16:06.830 --> 00:16:10.960
He had no computer model, and no mathematics of shock waves.

165
00:16:10.960 --> 00:16:15.090
But he refused to treat an ordinary sight as ordinary.

166
00:16:15.090 --> 00:16:21.780
He saw that the small rebellion on the wall of the glass was born of an imbalance between the two components of the mixture.

167
00:16:21.780 --> 00:16:26.070
Marangoni gave the idea a solid experimental foundation.

168
00:16:26.070 --> 00:16:30.520
Hosoi and Bush revealed the instability from which the droplets are born.

169
00:16:30.520 --> 00:16:37.530
And Bertozzi and her team showed that the climbing front carries one of the most subtle structures in all of applied mathematics.

170
00:16:37.530 --> 00:16:44.860
Over more than a century and a half, the same glass posed a different question to four generations of scientists.

171
00:16:44.860 --> 00:16:55.390
The next time you swirl your glass, that thin band climbing the wall will carry the trace not only of the wine, but of evaporating alcohol, of water pulling the surface toward itself,

172
00:16:55.390 --> 00:16:58.880
and of a shock front advancing against gravity.

173
00:16:58.880 --> 00:17:05.090
Cover the mouth of the glass and the show stops; uncover it and it begins again.

174
00:17:05.090 --> 00:17:08.740
You don't need a laboratory to see it with your own eyes.

175
00:17:08.740 --> 00:17:12.390
We have come to the end of today's episode of Nöron Bilim.

176
00:17:12.390 --> 00:17:15.240
Until we meet again in a new story.
